    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9edd7d74_2caefd78",
        "filename": "board/servo_micro/board.c",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 207,
      "author": {
        "id": 1291807
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-08-03T07:10:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The write and read use different I2C addresses.  The higher level functions like i2c_read8() don\u0027t support that.\n\nAt one point I actually did play around with having i2c_read8() to support a true read only operation, when given a negative offset.  It looked something like this (recreated for reference):\nhttps://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromiumos/platform/ec/+/1161741\n\nI don\u0027t know if there are legitimate uses for negative offsets though.  If so a separate arg indicating read only would be needed.\n\nWhen nsanders@ saw that while looking over my shoulder (before this was ready for review), he made a point of not liking that change \u0026 preferring direct use of i2c_xfer().  I felt the same way FWIW (preferring i2c_xfer() over complicating i2c_read8() and friends).\n\nAlso, while I don\u0027t think it matters if the I2C lock is held across all 3 reads, I do think it\u0027s correct to hold it across each write+read combo.  Currently i2c_read8(), i2c_write8(), and friends always obtain \u0026 release the lock, and it\u0027s not re-entrant.  So that would require further interface changes/complication to support i2c_write8() + i2c_read8() without releasing the lock between them.",
      "parentUuid": "37dbe124_751875d0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 207,
        "startChar": 7,
        "endLine": 207,
        "endChar": 15
      },
      "revId": "6971697b537e17cc65d1fa7c30773e384652c61f",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
